About StringTools

Before Maya came along, I used to do a lot of scripting on the Mac in an application called Frontier. It has a really nice language, not much different from MEL. Anyway, it had a really nice set of string functions that I rally got used to. Regex is nice and all, but it's very cryptic, difficult to remember and sometimes a real ass pain to use.
So, I converted all of Frontier's string functions to MEL. Well... as close as I possibly could. They mostly make use of regex and tokenize to get the job done, but now you don't have to remember the regex to get work done. I think they make working with strings a total snap and the read really well in your code.
Each function has complete usage description and examples in the header



String Matching:

stringAppearsInList			- does a string appear in a string array?
stringContains				- does a string appear in another string?



Word* functions:

stringFirstWord				- get the first word in a string
stringLastWord				- get the last word
stringNthWord				- get a word by word position
stringPopFirstWord			- delete the first word in a string
stringPopLastWord			- delete the last word in a string
stringPopNthWord			- delete a word by word position
stringInsertWordNth			- insert a word into a string by position
stringInsertWordMatch		- insert a word into a string by word match
stringSplitWordMatch		- split a string into an array or two strings by word match
stringSplitWordPosition		- split a string into an array of two string by word position
stringWordCount				- the number of words in the source string
stringWordPosition			- the position of a matched word in the source string



Character functions:

stringFirstChar				- get the first character of a string
stringLastChar				- get the last character
stringNthChar				- get a character by character position
stringPopFirstChar			- delete the first character in a string
stringPopLastChar			- delete the last character in a string
stringPopNthChar			- delete a character by character position
stringInsertCharPosition	- inster a string at a character position
stringTokenizeChars			- returns an array where each character has it's own index
stringCharPosition			- get the numeric position of a character in a string


*All word functions use a word character as the last argument. This is expected to be a single character such as a space (" "), underscore ("_"), or whatever separates words in the string. 